{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Escape 2 in this epic's Context was corrected after the fact: the original text said role-scoped simulate-principal-policy PROVED the planned IAM grant would have failed in production. It did not — that simulator conclusion was itself wrong, and only a real from-instance call settled it (the Converse API authorizes against bedrock:InvokeModel, not bedrock:Converse; see ticket 9249 for the full reversal).\n\nThis makes the example STRONGER, not weaker, and the epic text now reflects it: the investigation used two successive instruments, and NEITHER could observe the failure mode it was being used to rule out — a bedrock:* identity because it could not fail at all, and a policy simulator because it answers what the policy language permits for a NAMED action rather than which action the service CHECKS.\n\nDesign consequence for this epic's child story: the measurement-provenance contract should capture not only WHERE and UNDER WHAT PRIVILEGE a measurement was taken, but what KIND of instrument produced it (simulation / static analysis / live call) — because a simulation and a live call are not interchangeable evidence for an authorization claim, and the current attestation shape cannot tell them apart."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785432108703245001,"uuid":"ff80dc6a-4e65-43f8-895f-50fab023fc8e"}